1
00:00:04,134 --> 00:00:07,470
Before the law, a word about words.

2
00:00:09,235 --> 00:00:22,668
Some words we have heard so often that we no longer hear them. Say an eye for an
eye, and nearly everyone will tell you what it means: revenge, measured out exactly.

3
00:00:22,718 --> 00:00:37,874
Say the word free, and you will have to ask which kind. Free as in no charge, or
free as in no chains. The first costs nothing. The second can cost everything.

4
00:00:37,924 --> 00:00:49,408
Last time, one word in a law turned out to mean a fall and not a trip. This law has
both of these in it. An eye, and free.

5
00:00:49,458 --> 00:00:52,330
And one of them does not mean what most people think.

6
00:00:54,880 --> 00:01:01,230
Two verses of law, and in them the only price the Bible ever puts on an eye.

7
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:11,659
If a man strikes and blinds the eye of his manservant or maidservant, he must let
the servant go free as compensation for the eye.

8
00:01:11,709 --> 00:01:20,070
And if he knocks out the tooth of his manservant or maidservant, he must let the
servant go free as compensation for the tooth.

9
00:01:21,764 --> 00:01:30,450
And now, Mister Scaredy Cat. Shy Brian is back, with his own words and another man's
voice.

10
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:35,500
[Brian] Reading this law, the first thing that struck me was the pairing of an eye
with a tooth.

11
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:40,830
[Brian] I have lost two teeth since I lost my two eyes, and the adjustment was not
remotely the same.

12
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:51,370
[Brian] Put plainly: a servant with a loose tooth could be struck once and walk out
free, and the law sets that beside a man who will never see again. It seemed
unbalanced.

13
00:01:52,420 --> 00:02:04,530
An eye and a tooth, side by side, with the same price. This episode is about why,
and about what the price is, and what it is not.

14
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:17,159
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

15
00:02:17,209 --> 00:02:26,292
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

16
00:02:26,342 --> 00:02:37,127
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so. This episode is part of our series
on the Law: what God commanded about the blind.

17
00:02:37,177 --> 00:02:40,400
In these episodes there are no imagined scenes.

18
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:50,790
Everything you hear is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice,
from the Hebrew words themselves, and from dusty old Brian.

19
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:58,232
First, where we are. Exodus chapter twenty-one.

20
00:02:58,282 --> 00:03:06,191
Israel is at Sinai, in the first year out of Egypt, and these are the laws that
follow straight after the Ten Commandments.

21
00:03:06,241 --> 00:03:17,518
They are case laws: if this happens, then that. This stretch of them deals with
injuries. A servant beaten to death. A pregnant woman struck in a fight.

22
00:03:17,568 --> 00:03:29,250
And the rule of equal measure that everyone has heard of, eye for eye, tooth for
tooth. Our two verses come right after it. Exodus chapter 21, verses 20 to 27.

23
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:39,119
[Reader] If a man strikes his manservant or maidservant with a rod, and the servant
dies by his hand, he shall surely be punished.

24
00:03:39,169 --> 00:03:47,507
[Reader] However, if the servant gets up after a day or two, the owner shall not be
punished, since the servant is his property.

25
00:03:47,557 --> 00:03:55,729
[Reader] If men who are fighting strike a pregnant woman and her child is born
prematurely, but there is no further injury,

26
00:03:55,779 --> 00:04:01,499
[Reader] he shall surely be fined as the woman’s husband demands and as the court
allows.

27
00:04:01,549 --> 00:04:15,496
[Reader] But if a serious injury results, then you must require a life for a life—
eye for eye, tooth for tooth, hand for hand, foot for foot, burn for burn, wound for
wound,

28
00:04:15,546 --> 00:04:17,362
[Reader] and stripe for stripe.

29
00:04:17,412 --> 00:04:28,462
[Reader] If a man strikes and blinds the eye of his manservant or maidservant, he
must let the servant go free as compensation for the eye.

30
00:04:28,512 --> 00:04:35,990
[Reader] And if he knocks out the tooth of his manservant or maidservant, he must
let the servant go free as compensation for the tooth.

31
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:47,239
Two things stand out before we go any further. The law assumes that masters did
strike servants, and that eyes were lost that way.

32
00:04:47,289 --> 00:04:57,441
And it does not apply eye for eye to the master. The master does not lose his eye.
The servant gains his freedom.

33
00:04:57,491 --> 00:05:05,310
Whether that was more or less protection than a servant had in the lands around
Israel is a question the old books can answer, and we will get to it.

34
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:19,696
Now the words. There are two in these verses worth following, and a third that the
Law adds from elsewhere. The first is the word for eye: "ah-yin."

35
00:05:19,746 --> 00:05:32,216
Strong's dictionary gives it as an eye, and then adds something easy to miss in
English. By analogy, it says, a fountain. The eye of the landscape.

36
00:05:32,266 --> 00:05:41,770
The same word is a spring of water. The 1915 encyclopedia calls the eye the lamp of
the body, and then says this:

37
00:05:42,457 --> 00:05:51,950
[The old books] A cruel custom therefore sanctioned among heathen nations the
putting out of the eyes of an enemy or a rival, because thus his power was most
effectually shattered.

38
00:05:53,138 --> 00:06:04,150
The encyclopedia gives three examples from the Bible, and two of them are stories on
our site. The first is Samson. Judges chapter 16, verse 21.

39
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:17,470
[Reader] Then the Philistines seized him, gouged out his eyes, and brought him down
to Gaza, where he was bound with bronze shackles and forced to grind grain in the
prison.

40
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:27,990
The second is Zedekiah, the last king of Judah, after the fall of Jerusalem. 2 Kings
chapter 25, verse 7.

41
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:39,290
[Reader] And they slaughtered the sons of Zedekiah before his eyes. Then they put
out his eyes, bound him with bronze shackles, and took him to Babylon.

42
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:54,421
Gouged out. Put out. Those are the eyes of the Old Testament outside this law: taken
by enemies, to break a man. The law of the servant stands between those two men.

43
00:06:54,471 --> 00:07:01,570
It is the one place where the Law of Moses puts a price on an eye, and the price is
paid to the man who lost it.

44
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:06,830
The second word is the word for free: "khof-shee."

45
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:19,972
At the start of this episode we heard that free can mean two things: free as in no
charge, and free as in no chains. "Khof-shee" holds both.

46
00:07:20,022 --> 00:07:33,837
Strong's dictionary gives it as exempt: from bondage, from tax, or from care. A man
who is "khof-shee" owes nothing more, and belongs to no one.

47
00:07:33,887 --> 00:07:46,447
Hear the servant's law again with that in mind. He must let the servant go free as
compensation for the eye. The master does not hand him money for the eye.

48
00:07:46,497 --> 00:07:55,650
He pays with the man himself. Whatever time the servant still owed is cancelled, and
he walks out owing nothing.

49
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:06,998
And here is the thing. This is not a new word, made up for this one law. If you were
reading the chapter from the top, you have already met it.

50
00:08:07,048 --> 00:08:15,071
A few verses earlier, in the same chapter, the same word is used for the Hebrew
servant who goes free in the seventh year.

51
00:08:15,121 --> 00:08:25,630
Before the chapter says anything about eyes and teeth, it has already taught us what
"khof-shee" means. Exodus chapter 21, verse 2.

52
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:36,450
[Reader] If you buy a Hebrew servant, he is to serve you for six years. But in the
seventh year, he shall go free without paying anything.

53
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:46,250
Deuteronomy repeats the seventh-year rule forty years later, and adds something.
Deuteronomy chapter 15, verses 12 to 15.

54
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:56,259
[Reader] If a fellow Hebrew, a man or a woman, is sold to you and serves you six
years, then in the seventh year you must set him free.

55
00:08:56,309 --> 00:09:06,578
[Reader] And when you release him, do not send him away empty-handed. You are to
furnish him liberally from your flock, your threshing floor, and your winepress.

56
00:09:06,628 --> 00:09:10,027
[Reader] You shall give to him as the LORD your God has blessed you.

57
00:09:10,077 --> 00:09:18,110
[Reader] Remember that you were slaves in the land of Egypt, and the LORD your God
redeemed you; that is why I am giving you this command today.

58
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:26,940
Remember that you were slaves in Egypt. That is the reason the Law gives for freeing
a servant at all.

59
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:36,012
So when the law of the blinded servant says he goes free, "khof-shee", it is not
inventing a new kind of release.

60
00:09:36,062 --> 00:09:48,710
It is giving him the release the Law already knew, the seventh-year freedom, at
once. An eye bought what six years of service bought. A tooth bought it too.

61
00:09:49,740 --> 00:10:01,667
Brian remembered, while reading this, that there was a second release in the Law
besides the seventh year, and he was right. Every fiftieth year was the Jubilee.

62
00:10:01,717 --> 00:10:04,250
Leviticus chapter 25, verse 10.

63
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:12,419
[Reader] So you are to consecrate the fiftieth year and proclaim liberty in the land
for all its inhabitants.

64
00:10:12,469 --> 00:10:19,390
[Reader] It shall be your Jubilee, when each of you is to return to his property and
to his clan.

65
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:26,930
And for the man who had sold himself: Leviticus chapter 25, verses 39 to 41.

66
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:35,881
[Reader] If a countryman among you becomes destitute and sells himself to you, then
you must not force him into slave labor.

67
00:10:35,931 --> 00:10:44,161
[Reader] Let him stay with you as a hired worker or temporary resident; he is to
work for you until the Year of Jubilee.

68
00:10:44,211 --> 00:10:51,510
[Reader] Then he and his children are to be released, and he may return to his clan
and to the property of his fathers.

69
00:10:52,560 --> 00:11:05,790
The word for liberty there is a third word: "deh-rore." It is not the servant's
word, "khof-shee." It is a different word, and it turns up in one more place.

70
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:19,824
If you heard episode six, you have heard this verse before. Jesus stood up in the
synagogue at Nazareth, in His home town. He was handed the scroll of Isaiah.

71
00:11:19,874 --> 00:11:26,870
And this is the passage He found and read aloud. Isaiah chapter 61, verse 1.

72
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:36,627
[Reader] The Spirit of the Lord GOD is on Me, because the LORD has anointed Me to
preach good news to the poor.

73
00:11:36,677 --> 00:11:44,190
[Reader] He has sent Me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the
captives and freedom to the prisoners,

74
00:11:44,740 --> 00:12:00,450
To proclaim liberty to the captives. "Deh-rore". The Jubilee's word. The blinded
servant's release did not wait for the seventh year or the fiftieth. It came that
day.

75
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:08,456
Now the other law codes, because this is the one law where the comparison is exact.

76
00:12:08,506 --> 00:12:22,430
Hammurabi of Babylon, some three to five centuries before Moses, wrote laws about
eyes and teeth too. Here they are, in Johns's translation of 1903, read in order.

77
00:12:23,673 --> 00:12:35,919
[The old books] Section 196. If a man has caused the loss of a gentleman's eye, his
eye one shall cause to be lost. Section 198.

78
00:12:35,969 --> 00:12:48,472
[The old books] If he has caused a poor man to lose his eye or shattered a poor
man's limb, he shall pay one mina of silver. Section 199.

79
00:12:48,522 --> 00:13:02,026
[The old books] If he has caused the loss of the eye of a gentleman's servant or has
shattered the limb of a gentleman's servant, he shall pay half his price. Section
200.

80
00:13:02,076 --> 00:13:14,432
[The old books] If a man has made the tooth of a man that is his equal to fall out,
one shall make his tooth fall out. Section 201.

81
00:13:14,482 --> 00:13:21,430
[The old books] If he has made the tooth of a poor man to fall out, he shall pay
one-third of a mina of silver.

82
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:31,347
Hear the scale. A gentleman's eye costs you your own eye. A poor man's eye costs one
mina of silver.

83
00:13:31,397 --> 00:13:39,101
A servant's eye costs half the servant's price, and nothing in the text says the
servant sees any of it.

84
00:13:39,151 --> 00:13:44,911
The money goes where the loss was counted, and the loss was counted to the owner.

85
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:53,230
The 1915 encyclopedia, in an article by an Assyriologist, weighed Exodus against
Babylon and said this:

86
00:13:53,780 --> 00:14:03,448
[The old books] One can hardly assert that the parallels quoted are accidental, but
just as little could one say that they are directly taken from the Code;

87
00:14:03,498 --> 00:14:11,570
[The old books] for they bear quite a definite impression due to the Israelitish
culture, and numerous marked divergences also exist.

88
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:23,850
The servant's freedom is one of those divergences, and the largest. In Babylon a
servant's eye was priced. In Israel it was freed.

89
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:34,710
Now to Brian's question. Why the tooth? Matthew Henry, in seventeen hundred and six,
read the two verses together and saw it.

90
00:14:35,260 --> 00:14:44,627
[Matthew Henry] The care God took of servants. If their masters maimed them, though
it was only striking out a tooth, that should be their discharge.

91
00:14:44,677 --> 00:14:49,790
[Matthew Henry] To comfort them if they were abused; the loss of a limb should be
the gaining of their liberty,

92
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:54,410
[Matthew Henry] which would do something towards balancing both the pain and
disgrace they underwent.

93
00:14:55,460 --> 00:15:03,162
Though it was only striking out a tooth. The tooth is not in the law because it
equals an eye.

94
00:15:03,212 --> 00:15:09,228
It is in the law because it is the smallest lasting harm anyone would bother to
name.

95
00:15:09,278 --> 00:15:16,797
Set the threshold there, and every harm above it, the eye above all, is beyond
doubt.

96
00:15:16,847 --> 00:15:28,563
Smith's dictionary reads it the same way: the master's power over the servant's body
was so far limited that maiming gave liberty at once, by the fact itself.

97
00:15:28,613 --> 00:15:37,650
And by the first century, Josephus says, even eye for eye among free men had become
a matter of money, at the choice of the one who was hurt.

98
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:46,200
[The old books] He that maimeth any one, let him undergo the like himself, and be
deprived of the same member of which he hath deprived the other,

99
00:15:46,250 --> 00:15:54,870
[The old books] unless he that is maimed will accept of money instead of it; for the
law makes the sufferer the judge of the value of what he hath suffered.

100
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:03,150
The sufferer the judge of the value. Keep that phrase. It is what Brian is about to
do.

101
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:08,950
Brian, on the tooth, on the money, and on the other side of it.

102
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:14,189
[Brian] Reading this law, the first thing that struck me was the pairing of an eye
with a tooth.

103
00:16:14,239 --> 00:16:19,819
[Brian] I have lost two teeth since I lost my two eyes, and the adjustment was not
remotely the same.

104
00:16:19,869 --> 00:16:30,799
[Brian] Put plainly: a servant with a loose tooth could be struck once and walk out
free, and the law sets that beside a man who will never see again. It seemed
unbalanced.

105
00:16:30,849 --> 00:16:35,848
[Brian] Then I read it as the old commentators did. The tooth is not there because
it equals an eye.

106
00:16:35,898 --> 00:16:40,507
[Brian] It is there because it is the smallest lasting harm anyone would bother to
name.

107
00:16:40,557 --> 00:16:50,379
[Brian] If even that freed a servant, then nothing a master could do to a servant's
body was free of cost. The eye was never in question. The second thing was the
money.

108
00:16:50,429 --> 00:16:58,905
[Brian] The tables from Babylon we just heard put silver on an eye, less silver on a
tooth, and for a servant's eye, a payment to the owner.

109
00:16:58,955 --> 00:17:08,447
[Brian] How do you price reduced sight, or no sight at all? I would guess that some
of these servants were left completely blind. Is that worth the price of freedom?

110
00:17:08,497 --> 00:17:18,244
[Brian] The Law of Moses does not try to answer in silver. It names no sum. It gives
the servant the one thing the master cannot keep back, which is himself.

111
00:17:18,294 --> 00:17:23,256
[Brian] Here is the other side of it, and I mean this. I have lived without light
since 2014.

112
00:17:23,306 --> 00:17:30,355
[Brian] My freedom, and above all my freedom to worship God in the way I choose, is
worth more to me than my sight ever was.

113
00:17:30,405 --> 00:17:40,897
[Brian] I have adapted, adjusted, and found God's purpose for me. In so many ways my
blindness has been a gift from God, the third greatest after my salvation and my
wife.

114
00:17:40,947 --> 00:17:49,567
[Brian] It has brought us more blessings than I can list, and it has shaped my
character to fit His purposes better than it did. So I read this law two ways at
once.

115
00:17:49,617 --> 00:17:57,234
[Brian] Freedom is not a price for an eye, and the law does not pretend it is. But
freedom is not a small thing to hand a blind man, either.

116
00:17:57,284 --> 00:18:05,802
[Brian] I have never been a slave, and most blind people I know never will be. But
most of us have felt the shackles of blindness all the same.

117
00:18:05,852 --> 00:18:14,327
[Brian] In the early days it is a kind of prison. There are rooms you cannot cross
without help, and a world that stops where your hands can reach.

118
00:18:14,377 --> 00:18:18,273
[Brian] Other people decide where you go and when. I felt those bindings.

119
00:18:18,323 --> 00:18:24,837
[Brian] So when this law hands a blinded servant his freedom, I do not need to have
been a servant to understand what he was given.

120
00:18:24,887 --> 00:18:32,741
[Brian] I understand freedom, because I know what it is to lose it, and to be given
it back. That is how the gift I spoke of came to me.

121
00:18:32,791 --> 00:18:39,765
[Brian] It did not come all at once, and it did not come by getting my sight back.
It came by being set free inside the dark.

122
00:18:39,815 --> 00:18:45,610
[Brian] The servant in this law walked out the door the day he lost his eye. It took
me longer. But I walked out too.

123
00:18:54,860 --> 00:19:06,810
Next time, the one law that limits what a blind man may do: the blind priest, who
could not go near the altar, and who ate at the table all the same.

124
00:19:08,460 --> 00:19:17,574
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

125
00:19:17,624 --> 00:19:28,849
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

126
00:19:28,899 --> 00:19:36,490
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

